{
 "about": "A fictional four-generation family that kept an apple orchard. Nora is the person. No cluster opens at a death: a move across the country, the orchard's failure, her father's job loss and her mother-in-law moving into her house each open one, and her brother's wedding opens nothing. Her answer gives an age for one hard time of her own; she only wonders about two others.",
 "placeholder": "PLACEHOLDER: the key is written from the cluster rules, not from a real case. Patrick is the oracle and has not confirmed it (R-0856).",
 "status": "placeholder",
 "note": "Written by hand from the cluster rules page of 2026-10-14 and the fourteen decided conflicts; Patrick has not confirmed any of it. Four clusters, each opened by a change in the family that is not a death and named by that change and its years (A6). The move west, 1952-53, holds the shifts of the parents, the three children and Walter's brother left behind (A4: anyone's in the extended family). Losing the orchard, 1968-69, holds Carl's asthma easing while Nora's bedwetting began (a fall with another's rise is part of one movement) and Ida's heart trouble on her mother's side. The cannery layoff, 1975-76, is also the person's own period: every event of those two years. Wilma moving in, 1990-91, is an addition to the household. Events of unknown date sit inside every cluster as members and open none, and Wade's birth, of unknown date, comes just before the orchard failed and opens nothing (A9). The wildfire of August 1994 is an acute episode, five days of trouble around a real threat that soon passed, not a period: a try that stores it misses. Tess's drinking, 1996-97, has no change in the family beside it, so it is held as a hypothesis and scored neither way. June's arthritis from 1958 is a lasting complaint, coded as functioning down and never counted toward a period. Carl's wedding in 1984 has one shift beside it, and one shift alone is no period. Nothing is in the diagram from 1977 through 1983: a quiet time of seven years with no events.",
